INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team has developed a new feature engineering pipeline using AWS Glue for their fraud detection model. They need to ensure that the features generated during training are applied identically during real-time inference to prevent training-serving skew. The solution must provide a centralized, consistent, and low-latency way to retrieve features for both training and inference. Which AWS service should they use?', '["Store the features in Amazon S3 and load them directly into SageMaker for training and inference.","Re-run the AWS Glue job at inference time to generate features on-demand for each prediction.","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to store and serve pre-computed features consistently for training and inference.","Use AWS Lambda to transform raw data into features during inference, mirroring the Glue job logic."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the ideal solution. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store is specifically designed to store, share, and manage features for machine learning. It provides a centralized repository that ensures features used for training are identical to those used for inference, preventing training-serving skew ([ap-1.2-002]). It offers both online (low-latency) and offline (batch) stores for feature retrieval. Option A (incorrect) Storing features in Amazon S3 is common, but it doesn''t inherently guarantee consistency between training and inference feature generation, nor does it provide low-latency retrieval for real-time inference without additional custom development. Option B (incorrect) Re-running an AWS Glue job at inference time for each prediction is highly inefficient and would introduce unacceptable latency for real-time inference. This approach is an anti-pattern ([ap-1.2-002]) for maintaining feature consistency. Option D (incorrect) While AWS Lambda can perform transformations, replicating complex AWS Glue job logic in Lambda for real-time inference is prone to errors, difficult to maintain, and risks introducing training-serving skew ([ap-1.2-002]) if the logic diverges. SageMaker Feature Store provides a managed solution for this consistency.', 14, 'm_14_2', 15, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A machine learning engineer is preparing a dataset for a regression model. The dataset contains numerical features with varying scales, and normalization is required. To prevent data leakage and ensure the model''s generalization ability, the engineer must apply normalization correctly. Which approach correctly handles normalization in a train-test split scenario?', '["Apply normalization to the entire dataset before splitting it into training and test sets.","Fit the scaler on the entire dataset, then transform both training and test sets separately.","Fit the scaler only on the training data, then transform both the training data and the test data using the fitted scaler.","Apply different normalization techniques to the training and test sets based on their individual distributions."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the correct approach to prevent data leakage. The scaler (e.g., StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler) must be fitted exclusively on the training data. This ensures that the statistics (mean, standard deviation, min/max) used for normalization are learned only from the training set. Subsequently, both the training data and the test data are transformed using these same statistics from the fitted scaler. Option A (incorrect) Applying normalization to the entire dataset before splitting is an anti-pattern ([ap-1.2-001]). This causes data leakage because the normalization parameters (e.g., mean, standard deviation) are influenced by the test data, making the model''s performance on the test set unrealistically optimistic. Option B (incorrect) Fitting the scaler on the entire dataset and then transforming separately still leads to data leakage, as the test data''s statistics are used to determine the scaler''s parameters. The fitting must only occur on the training data. Option D (incorrect) Applying different normalization techniques or fitting separate scalers for training and test sets would introduce inconsistencies and lead to training-serving skew, making the model''s evaluation unreliable and potentially causing issues in production.', 14, 'm_14_2', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team is preparing a large, raw dataset for a new ML model. The data requires extensive cleaning, transformation, and feature engineering, including handling missing values, encoding categorical variables, and creating new features from existing ones. They need a scalable, serverless solution that can process petabytes of data efficiently. Which AWS service is best suited for this comprehensive ETL and feature engineering task?', '["Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler for interactive data preparation and feature engineering with a visual interface.","Amazon Athena for querying raw data in Amazon S3 and performing simple transformations.","AWS Glue for serverless, Apache Spark-based ETL jobs to transform large datasets and perform feature engineering.","AWS Lambda functions to process and transform data files as they arrive in Amazon S3."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most suitable service for large-scale, comprehensive ETL and feature engineering. AWS Glue is a serverless data integration service that uses Apache Spark for highly scalable batch processing. It can handle petabytes of data, perform complex transformations, clean data, handle missing values, and engineer features efficiently, making it ideal for robust data pipelines. Option A (incorrect) Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler is excellent for interactive, visual data preparation and feature engineering, especially for data scientists. While powerful, for very large-scale, scheduled, and automated ETL pipelines, AWS Glue is generally more robust and cost-effective. Option B (incorrect) Amazon Athena is a query service for data in Amazon S3. While it can perform simple transformations via SQL, it is not designed for complex, multi-step ETL and feature engineering pipelines on petabytes of data, especially for tasks like encoding categorical variables or handling missing values programmatically. Option D (incorrect) AWS Lambda functions are suitable for event-driven, smaller-scale data processing. They have execution duration limits and are not designed for complex, large-scale batch ETL and feature engineering jobs that require the distributed processing power of Apache Spark.', 14, 'm_14_2', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data scientist is working with a dataset that contains numerous missing values in several important features. Before training an ML model, these missing values must be handled to prevent errors and improve model performance. The goal is to fill in the missing data using a statistically sound method to minimize bias. Which technique is appropriate for handling missing data?', '["Dropping all rows or columns that contain any missing values, regardless of the amount of missing data.","Imputing missing numerical values with the mean, median, or mode of the respective feature.","Using one-hot encoding for all features, which automatically handles missing values.","Replacing missing values with a constant placeholder like ''NA'' or -1, then training the model."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is a common and statistically sound technique for handling missing numerical data. Imputing missing values with the mean, median, or mode of the feature can help preserve data integrity and prevent information loss, especially when the amount of missing data is not excessive. The choice between mean, median, or mode depends on the data distribution (e.g., median for skewed data). Option A (incorrect) Dropping all rows or columns with missing values can lead to significant data loss, especially in datasets with many features or sparse missingness, potentially removing valuable information and reducing the effective size of the dataset. Option C (incorrect) One-hot encoding is for categorical variables and does not automatically handle missing values in a statistically sound way; missing values in categorical features would typically need to be treated as a separate category or imputed before encoding. Option D (incorrect) Replacing missing values with a constant placeholder like ''NA'' or -1 can introduce artificial patterns or biases into the data, misleading the model into interpreting the placeholder as a meaningful value, which can negatively impact model performance.', 14, 'm_14_2', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A large enterprise has multiple data science teams working on various ML projects, often requiring access to the same pre-processed features (e.g., customer demographics, product embeddings). They need a centralized repository to store, share, and manage these features to promote reusability, ensure consistency across models, and accelerate model development. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?', '["Amazon S3 to store feature datasets in separate buckets for each team.","AWS Glue Data Catalog to register feature schemas and locations in Amazon S3.","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to centralize, manage, and serve features for training and inference.","Amazon Redshift to store all features in a data warehouse for querying."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the purpose-built service for this scenario. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store provides a centralized repository for storing, managing, and sharing features across multiple ML projects and teams. It ensures consistency between features used for training and inference, promotes reusability, and offers both online (low-latency) and offline (batch) stores for feature retrieval, significantly accelerating model development. Option A (incorrect) While Amazon S3 can store feature datasets, it doesn''t provide the managed capabilities for feature versioning, consistency, or easy sharing across teams that SageMaker Feature Store offers. It would require significant custom development to replicate these functionalities. Option B (incorrect) AWS Glue Data Catalog is useful for metadata management and schema discovery but does not provide the full lifecycle management, online/offline stores, or consistency guarantees for ML features that SageMaker Feature Store offers. Option D (incorrect) Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse for analytical queries. While it can store features, it''s not optimized for the specific requirements of feature management for ML, such as ensuring consistency between training and inference or providing low-latency online feature serving.', 14, 'm_14_2', 19, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team is building a classification model for a rare event, resulting in a highly imbalanced dataset where the minority class is significantly underrepresented. Training a model directly on this dataset often leads to poor performance on the minority class. They need to apply a technique to address this class imbalance to improve the model''s ability to predict the rare event. Which technique is appropriate?', '["Dropping all instances of the majority class to balance the dataset.","Applying Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to reduce the dimensionality of the features.","Using a random split for the dataset to ensure unbiased distribution.","Employing oversampling techniques like SMOTE or undersampling the majority class."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the appropriate technique for handling class imbalance. Oversampling techniques, such as Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE), create synthetic samples for the minority class, while undersampling techniques reduce the number of samples in the majority class. Both aim to balance the class distribution, allowing the model to learn more effectively from the minority class and improve its predictive performance for rare events. Option A (incorrect) Dropping all instances of the majority class would lead to significant loss of information and is generally not a viable strategy, as the model would lose context from the more common class. Option B (incorrect) Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a dimensionality reduction technique, which helps in reducing the number of features but does not directly address class imbalance. Option C (incorrect) Using a random split for an imbalanced dataset can exacerbate the problem, as it might lead to training or validation sets with even fewer (or no) minority class samples, making it harder for the model to learn. Stratified splitting is preferred for imbalanced data.', 14, 'm_14_2', 20, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team is training a machine learning model with a dataset containing hundreds of features, many of which are highly correlated or provide redundant information. This high dimensionality is leading to increased training times, overfitting, and reduced model interpretability. They need to reduce the number of features while retaining most of the important information. Which technique should they apply?', '["Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to transform the features into a smaller set of uncorrelated components.","One-hot encoding to convert all numerical features into categorical representations.","Standardization of numerical features to bring them to a common scale.","Imputing missing values in the dataset using the mean or median of each feature."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate technique for reducing dimensionality while retaining important information. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a feature selection and dimensionality reduction technique that transforms a set of possibly correlated features into a smaller set of uncorrelated features called principal components, capturing most of the variance in the original data. This helps reduce training times, mitigate overfitting, and improve model interpretability. Option B (incorrect) One-hot encoding is a technique for converting categorical features into a numerical format, not for dimensionality reduction of existing numerical features. In fact, it can increase dimensionality. Option C (incorrect) Standardization (e.g., using StandardScaler) scales numerical features to have a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1. While important for many ML algorithms, it does not reduce the number of features or address multicollinearity. Option D (incorrect) Imputing missing values is a data cleaning step to handle incomplete data, not a technique for dimensionality reduction.', 14, 'm_14_2', 21, NULL, NULL);

