INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team is developing a new fraud detection model using a deep neural network. During initial training, the model achieves 98% accuracy on the training set but only 70% on the validation set, indicating significant overfitting. The team needs to address this issue with the LEAST operational overhead and without collecting additional data. Which approach should they implement?', '["Implement early stopping during SageMaker Training Jobs and add L1/L2 regularization to the model architecture.","Manually reduce the model''s complexity by decreasing the number of layers and neurons, then retrain the model.","Increase the size of the training dataset by generating synthetic data to provide more diverse examples to the model.","Utilize SageMaker Clarify to analyze feature importance and remove less significant features from the input data."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) directly addresses overfitting with minimal operational overhead. Early stopping is a built-in technique in many training frameworks that stops training when validation performance plateaus or degrades, and L1/L2 regularization can be easily added to the model definition. These are standard and efficient methods to combat overfitting without requiring manual architectural changes or data generation. Option B (incorrect) involves manual trial-and-error to reduce model complexity, which is time-consuming and increases operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) suggests generating synthetic data, which is a complex process and goes against the constraint of not collecting additional data, and is often a more involved solution than regularization. Option D (incorrect) uses SageMaker Clarify for feature importance, which is useful for explainability and bias detection, but not a primary or direct method to resolve overfitting during training, and removing features might not be sufficient.', 15, 'm_15_3', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A financial institution is developing a machine learning model to approve loan applications. Before deploying the model to production, the data science team needs to ensure the model does not exhibit unfair bias against specific demographic groups based on features like age or income. They require a solution to analyze potential pre-training and post-training bias and provide explainability for model decisions. Which AWS service should they use?', '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Debugger to monitor the model''s performance metrics and identify any anomalies during training.","Leverage Amazon SageMaker Clarify to detect bias in the dataset and model predictions, and generate feature attribution reports.","Implement SageMaker Experiments to track different model versions and compare their performance across various demographic slices.","Deploy the model to a SageMaker endpoint and manually analyze prediction outcomes for different demographic groups."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Amazon SageMaker Clarify is specifically designed to help detect potential bias in machine learning models and datasets, and to provide explainability for model predictions. It can analyze pre-training data for imbalances and post-training predictions for disparate impact across different sensitive attributes. Option A (incorrect) uses SageMaker Debugger, which is focused on identifying issues during the training process, such as vanishing gradients or overfitting, not for bias detection or explainability. Option C (incorrect) uses SageMaker Experiments for tracking and comparing model runs, which is valuable for MLOps but does not inherently provide bias detection or feature attribution capabilities. Option D (incorrect) suggests manual analysis, which is inefficient, prone to human error, and does not provide the systematic, comprehensive reports offered by SageMaker Clarify.', 15, 'm_15_3', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data scientist is training a complex deep learning model on Amazon SageMaker. During training, they observe that the model''s loss is not decreasing as expected, and suspect issues like vanishing or exploding gradients. They need a way to proactively monitor internal model parameters and identify such anomalies in real-time without interrupting the training job. Which SageMaker feature should they use?', '["Configure SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (AMT) to automatically adjust hyperparameters and resolve training issues.","Implement SageMaker Experiments to log all training metrics and manually review them for anomalies after the job completes.","Integrate Amazon SageMaker Debugger with custom rules to monitor tensors like gradients and weights during the training job.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms to trigger when the training job''s CPU utilization drops below a certain threshold."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective solution. Amazon SageMaker Debugger is designed to analyze training jobs in real-time, allowing data scientists to inspect internal model tensors (like gradients, weights, and biases) and detect common issues such as vanishing/exploding gradients, overfitting, or underfitting. It can be configured with built-in or custom rules to automatically detect anomalies and provide alerts. Option A (incorrect) uses SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (AMT) for hyperparameter optimization, which aims to find the best model configuration, but it does not directly diagnose or resolve internal training issues like gradient problems. Option B (incorrect) uses SageMaker Experiments for logging, which is useful for tracking, but requires manual post-job analysis and doesn''t provide real-time anomaly detection. Option D (incorrect) uses CloudWatch for CPU utilization, which is a system-level metric and not directly indicative of internal model training issues like gradient problems.', 15, 'm_15_3', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A healthcare company is building a binary classification model to predict the likelihood of a rare disease. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with only 2% of cases being positive. The business objective is to minimize false negatives, as missing a positive case has severe consequences, even if it means a higher rate of false positives. Which evaluation metric should the data scientist prioritize to assess the model''s effectiveness?', '["Accuracy, as it provides a general measure of correct predictions across all classes.","Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC), to evaluate the model''s ability to distinguish between classes.","F1-score, to balance precision and recall, especially in imbalanced datasets.","Recall (Sensitivity), to ensure that a high proportion of actual positive cases are correctly identified."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most appropriate metric. Recall (also known as Sensitivity) measures the proportion of actual positive cases that are correctly identified. In a scenario where minimizing false negatives is critical (i.e., not missing a rare disease), maximizing recall is paramount, even if it leads to a higher number of false positives. Option A (incorrect) is accuracy, which can be misleading in imbalanced datasets because a model that always predicts the majority class can still achieve high accuracy while performing poorly on the minority class. Option B (incorrect) is AUC-ROC, which is a good overall measure for classification performance across various thresholds but doesn''t directly prioritize minimizing false negatives over false positives. Option C (incorrect) is F1-score, which is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, providing a balance. While useful for imbalanced datasets, the business objective explicitly prioritizes recall, making it the most direct metric to optimize.', 15, 'm_15_3', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A machine learning engineer has trained a new regression model to predict customer churn. After evaluating the model, they observe that both the training error and validation error are high, and the model''s performance is poor on unseen data. This indicates that the model is underfitting the data. Which initial action should the engineer take to address this issue with the LEAST effort?', '["Increase the complexity of the model by adding more layers or features, or try a more powerful algorithm.","Reduce the amount of training data to simplify the learning task for the model.","Apply stronger regularization techniques (e.g., L1/L2 regularization) to prevent the model from memorizing the training data.","Tune the hyperparameters using SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (AMT) with a very wide search space for all parameters."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most direct and often effective initial action for underfitting. Underfitting occurs when the model is too simple to capture the underlying patterns in the data. Increasing model complexity (e.g., adding more layers to a neural network, more features, or using a more sophisticated algorithm like XGBoost instead of Linear Learner) allows it to learn more intricate relationships. Option B (incorrect) would exacerbate underfitting by providing less data for the model to learn from. Option C (incorrect) applies stronger regularization, which is used to combat overfitting (when the model is too complex), not underfitting. Option D (incorrect) suggests using SageMaker AMT with a very wide search space, which is an inefficient approach (anti-pattern ap-2.2-001) and might not resolve underfitting if the model architecture itself is too simple.', 15, 'm_15_3', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A retail company has developed a recommendation engine that suggests products to customers. Business stakeholders are requesting insights into why certain products are recommended, especially for high-value customers, to ensure transparency and trust. The data science team needs to generate feature attribution scores for the model''s predictions to explain which input features contributed most to each recommendation. Which AWS service can provide this capability post-training?', '["Use Amazon SageMaker Debugger to analyze the model''s internal parameters during inference to understand feature contributions.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Clarify to generate feature attribution reports and explain model predictions.","Leverage SageMaker Experiments to log feature importance metrics calculated during model training.","Manually implement a SHAP or LIME library within the inference code deployed on a SageMaker endpoint."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most suitable service. Amazon SageMaker Clarify provides capabilities for model explainability, including generating feature attribution reports (e.g., using SHAP or LIME algorithms) to show which input features contributed most to a model''s prediction. This directly addresses the requirement for transparency and understanding why specific recommendations are made. Option A (incorrect) uses SageMaker Debugger, which is focused on debugging training jobs by monitoring internal tensors, not for post-training explainability of predictions. Option C (incorrect) uses SageMaker Experiments for tracking training runs and metrics, but it does not inherently provide feature attribution for individual predictions. Option D (incorrect) involves manual implementation of explainability libraries, which increases operational overhead and development effort compared to the managed capabilities of SageMaker Clarify.', 15, 'm_15_3', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A security company is deploying a machine learning model to detect intrusion attempts. The model outputs a probability score, and the team needs to set a classification threshold. It is crucial to detect as many actual intrusion attempts as possible, even if it leads to some false alarms, because missing a real threat is unacceptable. Which strategy should the team use to optimize the model''s threshold based on a confusion matrix?', '["Adjust the threshold to maximize the overall accuracy of the model on the validation set.","Set the threshold to balance precision and recall, aiming for a high F1-score.","Lower the classification threshold to increase the number of true positives (recall) at the expense of precision.","Raise the classification threshold to increase the number of true negatives (specificity) and reduce false positives."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the appropriate strategy. To detect as many actual intrusion attempts as possible (i.e., minimize false negatives), the team needs to prioritize recall. Lowering the classification threshold means that the model will classify more instances as positive, thereby increasing the true positive rate (recall) but potentially also increasing the false positive rate (reducing precision). Option A (incorrect) focuses on accuracy, which can be misleading in scenarios where one class is much more critical than the other. Option B (incorrect) aims for a balanced F1-score, which is not the primary objective when minimizing false negatives is paramount. Option D (incorrect) would raise the threshold, which would decrease false positives but also decrease true positives (recall), directly contradicting the business objective of detecting as many actual threats as possible.', 15, 'm_15_3', 7, NULL, NULL);

