INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team needs to deploy a new model for real-time inference. They want to automate infrastructure provisioning and ensure version control for the model artifacts and deployment configuration. The primary goal is to achieve the LEAST operational overhead for infrastructure management and model governance.', '["Use AWS CloudFormation to define SageMaker endpoint resources and integrate with Amazon SageMaker Model Registry for model versioning and approval.","Manually deploy models to Amazon SageMaker, storing artifacts in Amazon S3 buckets with versioning enabled.","Use AWS CDK to programmatically define SageMaker resources in a familiar programming language and manage model versions via Git tags in a source control repository.","Containerize models with Docker, push to Amazon ECR, and deploy to SageMaker via custom Python scripts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides a fully managed and integrated solution. AWS CloudFormation allows declarative infrastructure provisioning, while Amazon SageMaker Model Registry offers built-in model versioning, lineage tracking, and approval workflows, significantly reducing custom development and operational overhead for model governance. Option B (incorrect) involves manual processes and lacks integrated governance and approval workflows, increasing operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) uses AWS CDK for infrastructure, which is good, but managing model versions solely via Git tags for artifacts lacks the integrated approval and lineage tracking of SageMaker Model Registry, requiring more custom development for governance. Option D (incorrect) requires custom scripting for deployment, which increases operational overhead compared to declarative infrastructure as code and integrated model governance.', 16, 'm_16_2', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team needs to manage multiple versions of ML models and ensure traceability and approval before deployment to production. They want to automate this process with the LEAST manual effort, integrating it into their existing SageMaker workflows.', '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Model Registry to version models and implement approval workflows.","Store model artifacts in Amazon S3 with versioning enabled and track approvals manually in a spreadsheet.","Develop a custom database solution on Amazon RDS to store model metadata and manage approval statuses.","Deploy models directly from SageMaker training jobs to endpoints and rely on Git for version control of training code."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most efficient solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Registry provides a centralized repository for model versions, allowing for lineage tracking, metadata management, and built-in approval workflows. This significantly reduces manual effort and enhances governance. Option B (incorrect) involves manual tracking, which increases operational overhead and is prone to errors, failing the ''LEAST manual effort'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) requires developing and maintaining a custom database solution, which introduces significant operational overhead compared to a managed service like SageMaker Model Registry. Option D (incorrect) lacks formal model versioning and an explicit approval workflow for the deployed model artifacts themselves, representing a manual deployment anti-pattern.', 16, 'm_16_2', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'An ML engineering team wants to establish a CI/CD pipeline for their SageMaker models. They need to automatically build and test new model artifacts whenever code changes are pushed to a Git repository, ensuring quality before deployment.', '["Manually trigger SageMaker training jobs and evaluations after each code commit.","Configure an AWS CodePipeline with AWS CodeBuild to automate the build, test, and package steps for model artifacts.","Set up a cron job on an Amazon EC2 instance to periodically pull code and run build scripts.","Use AWS Lambda functions to monitor Git repositories and orchestrate custom build processes."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides a robust and automated CI/CD solution for ML models. AWS CodePipeline orchestrates the workflow, and AWS CodeBuild handles the compilation, testing, and packaging of model artifacts, integrating seamlessly with source control. Option A (incorrect) is a manual process that does not meet the automation requirement of a CI/CD pipeline. Option C (incorrect) is less scalable, introduces operational overhead for managing the EC2 instance, and is not a native CI/CD solution. Option D (incorrect) would be overly complex to manage for a full CI/CD pipeline, as Lambda functions have execution limits and managing complex build environments within them is challenging.', 16, 'm_16_2', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A development team is building a new ML model and needs to containerize it for deployment on Amazon SageMaker. They require a secure, scalable, and fully managed repository for their Docker images, integrated with AWS services.', '["Store the Docker image artifacts directly in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Push the Docker image to Amazon ECR (Elastic Container Registry).","Set up a local Docker registry on an Amazon EC2 instance.","Use a custom third-party container registry hosted outside of AWS."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the standard and most efficient approach. Amazon ECR is a fully managed Docker container registry that integrates seamlessly with Amazon SageMaker and other AWS services, providing secure, scalable, and reliable storage for container images. Option A (incorrect) is not suitable for storing Docker images directly; S3 is an object storage service, not a container registry. Option C (incorrect) introduces operational overhead for managing the EC2 instance and the registry software, failing the ''fully managed'' aspect. Option D (incorrect) would involve managing external infrastructure and potentially introduce security and integration complexities.', 16, 'm_16_2', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'An organization wants to define and manage their entire SageMaker ML workflow, including data processing, training, model evaluation, and conditional registration, as a series of interconnected steps using a declarative approach. They need a fully managed service for this orchestration.', '["Implement the workflow using Amazon SageMaker Pipelines.","Orchestrate the workflow using AWS Step Functions.","Write custom orchestration logic using AWS Lambda functions.","Manually execute each step of the ML workflow sequentially."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is specifically designed for this purpose. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines allows you to create, manage, and automate end-to-end ML workflows as a series of interconnected steps, supporting conditional logic and providing a declarative way to define the entire ML lifecycle. Option B (incorrect) is a general-purpose workflow orchestration service, but SageMaker Pipelines offers ML-specific integrations and components that are more tailored for ML workflows. Option C (incorrect) would require significant custom development and maintenance for state management and error handling, increasing operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) is a manual approach and does not meet the requirement for a managed, automated, and declarative workflow.', 16, 'm_16_2', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A data science team is developing a new model that requires custom pre-processing logic and a specific deep learning framework not natively supported by SageMaker. They need to package their model and dependencies for deployment to a SageMaker endpoint.', '["Use a pre-built Amazon SageMaker inference image and install custom dependencies at runtime.","Create a custom Docker image, push it to Amazon ECR, and use it with SageMaker for inference.","Deploy the model as an AWS Lambda function and manage all dependencies within the Lambda package.","Store the model artifacts and custom scripts in Amazon S3 and load them dynamically at inference time."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the recommended approach for custom environments. Creating a custom Docker image allows the team to include specific frameworks, libraries, and pre-processing logic, ensuring the exact environment is available for inference. Pushing it to Amazon ECR provides a secure and scalable registry for SageMaker to pull from. Option A (incorrect) might not be feasible if the framework is not supported, and installing dependencies at runtime can lead to slow cold starts and increased complexity. Option C (incorrect) has payload size limits and execution duration limits, making it unsuitable for complex ML models and large inference requests. Option D (incorrect) does not provide a controlled environment for dependencies and custom logic, making deployment and reproducibility challenging.', 16, 'm_16_2', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'A company needs to ensure that only approved model versions are deployed to production. They want to implement a governance process that includes versioning, approval, and lineage tracking for their ML models, with minimal custom development.', '["Store model artifacts in Amazon S3 with object versioning and manage approvals through a ticketing system.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Model Registry to manage model versions, metadata, and approval statuses.","Develop a custom model management system using Amazon DynamoDB for versioning and AWS Lambda for approval logic.","Deploy models directly from SageMaker training jobs without a centralized registry, relying on Git for code versioning."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective and managed solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Registry provides a centralized repository for model versions, offering built-in features for metadata tracking, lineage, and explicit approval workflows, which is crucial for governance with minimal custom development. Option A (incorrect) lacks formal approval workflows and lineage tracking for the models themselves, relying on manual processes that are prone to errors and do not meet the ''minimal custom development'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) requires significant custom development and maintenance to replicate the features already available in SageMaker Model Registry. Option D (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern of manually deploying models without a model registry or approval workflow, leading to a lack of governance and traceability.', 16, 'm_16_2', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774921932068', 'An ML engineering team is setting up a continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipeline for their SageMaker models. They need to define the infrastructure for their SageMaker resources (endpoints, models, pipelines) using a declarative approach, ensuring version control and easy replication.', '["Manually provision all SageMaker resources through the AWS Management Console.","Write custom Python scripts to create and update SageMaker resources.","Use AWS CloudFormation or AWS CDK to define SageMaker resources as infrastructure as code.","Store configuration details in a database and use AWS Lambda to deploy resources."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the best practice for defining infrastructure in a CI/CD pipeline. AWS CloudFormation and AWS CDK allow you to define SageMaker resources (like endpoints, models, and pipelines) as infrastructure as code (IaC), enabling version control, automated deployment, and easy replication across environments. Option A (incorrect) is a manual process that is error-prone, lacks version control, and cannot be easily replicated. Option B (incorrect) requires custom scripting and state management, which is less robust and harder to maintain than declarative IaC. Option D (incorrect) adds unnecessary complexity for infrastructure definition and deployment compared to dedicated IaC tools.', 16, 'm_16_2', 8, NULL, NULL);

