INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A data science team has deployed several Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoints for their fraud detection model. They need to continuously monitor the underlying instance resource utilization, such as CPU and memory, along with network throughput. The team requires automated notifications when resource usage exceeds predefined thresholds to proactively manage costs and optimize instance types with the LEAST operational overhead.', '["Deploy a comprehensive third-party monitoring agent on all SageMaker endpoint instances to collect detailed system metrics, including CPU, memory, and network, and send them to an external dashboard for visualization, advanced analytics, and custom alerting configurations, requiring significant integration effort.","Implement custom scripts on the SageMaker endpoint instances to collect resource metrics, push them to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, and then use CloudWatch Logs Insights for analysis and manual alerting.","Use Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect performance degradation and data drift, which will indirectly indicate high resource utilization.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch metrics for SageMaker endpoint instances and create CloudWatch Alarms based on CPUUtilization, MemoryUtilization, and NetworkThroughput, linking alarms to Amazon SNS."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) provides the most efficient solution with the least operational overhead. Amazon CloudWatch automatically collects standard metrics for Amazon SageMaker endpoint instances, including CPUUtilization, MemoryUtilization, and NetworkThroughput. CloudWatch Alarms can then be configured on these metrics to trigger automated notifications via Amazon SNS when thresholds are breached, requiring minimal setup and maintenance. Option A (incorrect) involves deploying and integrating a third-party monitoring agent, which significantly increases operational overhead and complexity. Option B (incorrect) requires custom scripting and manual setup to push metrics to CloudWatch Logs, which is more complex and has higher operational overhead than using native CloudWatch metrics. Option C (incorrect) is designed for monitoring model-specific metrics like data quality and model quality, not infrastructure resource utilization, and would not directly provide the required alerts for CPU, memory, or network throughput.', 17, 'm_17_2', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A large organization with multiple data science teams uses Amazon SageMaker extensively for model training and deployment. They need to accurately track and allocate costs for individual SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints to specific projects and departments. Which approach provides the MOST effective cost allocation and visibility?', '["Implement a detailed naming convention for all SageMaker resources, including training jobs, endpoints, and associated S3 buckets, ensuring that project and team identifiers are consistently embedded, and then manually filter and analyze costs in AWS Cost Explorer based on these naming patterns.","Develop custom scripts to extract SageMaker resource usage data from AWS CloudTrail logs, process it, and generate cost reports for each project.","Create separate AWS accounts for each project and team, then use AWS Organizations to consolidate billing and review costs per account.","Apply AWS resource tags with project and team keys/values to all SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints, then use AWS Cost Explorer to filter and group costs by these tags."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most effective method for granular cost allocation and visibility. AWS resource tags allow teams to categorize resources by project, department, or any other custom dimension. AWS Cost Explorer can then be used to filter, group, and analyze costs based on these tags, providing accurate and detailed cost breakdowns. Option A (incorrect) relies on naming conventions, which are prone to inconsistencies and are less flexible for detailed reporting compared to tags. Option B (incorrect) requires significant custom development and maintenance, increasing operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) is a valid strategy for strong isolation and billing, but for cost allocation within a single account or across consolidated billing, tagging is more granular and flexible for individual resources.', 17, 'm_17_2', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A data science team deploys several Amazon SageMaker inference endpoints for real-time predictions. The team observes that some endpoints are over-provisioned, leading to unnecessary costs, while others experience occasional performance bottlenecks during peak hours. The company needs a solution to optimize costs for these endpoints while maintaining performance, with the LEAST operational overhead.', '["Configure Amazon SageMaker endpoint auto-scaling based on Amazon CloudWatch metrics like CPU Utilization and InvocationsPerInstance.","Manually adjust instance types and counts for each Amazon SageMaker endpoint weekly based on cost reports and performance logs.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to monitor endpoint utilization and scale endpoints using the Amazon SageMaker API.","Migrate all Amazon SageMaker endpoints to AWS Lambda functions to leverage serverless scaling and reduce costs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most effective and operationally efficient solution. Amazon SageMaker endpoints support auto-scaling, which automatically adjusts the number of instances based on Amazon CloudWatch metrics such as CPU Utilization, Memory Utilization, and InvocationsPerInstance. This ensures that endpoints are right-sized for current demand, optimizing costs during low utilization periods and maintaining performance during peak loads with minimal manual intervention. Option B (incorrect) involves significant manual effort, which directly violates the ''LEAST operational overhead'' constraint and is prone to human error. Option C (incorrect) introduces custom development and maintenance overhead for the AWS Lambda function, which is more complex than leveraging SageMaker''s built-in auto-scaling capabilities. Option D (incorrect) is not a direct or always feasible solution; migrating complex SageMaker models to AWS Lambda for inference can be challenging due to package size limits, cold start issues, and potential performance differences, and it doesn''t directly address the SageMaker endpoint optimization requirement.', 17, 'm_17_2', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A machine learning engineering team has deployed a critical Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint. They need to monitor the endpoint''s operational health, specifically focusing on latency and throughput, to ensure it meets strict service level agreements (SLAs). The solution must provide automated alerts if performance degrades, with the LEAST amount of custom development.', '["Develop a custom monitoring agent to run on the SageMaker instance, collecting metrics and sending them to an external monitoring system.","Utilize Amazon CloudWatch metrics for Amazon SageMaker endpoints, such as `Invocations` and `ModelLatency`, and configure Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Periodically query the Amazon SageMaker endpoint directly from an AWS Lambda function to measure response times and log results to Amazon S3.","Implement AWS CloudTrail to log all API calls to the SageMaker endpoint and analyze the logs for performance issues."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and low-overhead solution. Amazon SageMaker automatically publishes a rich set of operational metrics, including `Invocations` (throughput) and `ModelLatency`, to Amazon CloudWatch. Configuring Amazon CloudWatch Alarms on these metrics allows the team to receive automated notifications when performance thresholds are breached, requiring no custom development. Option A (incorrect) involves significant custom development and ongoing maintenance for a monitoring agent, which violates the ''LEAST custom development'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) also requires custom development for the AWS Lambda function and introduces additional latency and cost for polling, which is less efficient than using native CloudWatch metrics. Option D (incorrect) is primarily for auditing and governance, logging API calls, but it does not provide real-time performance metrics like latency and throughput in a readily consumable format for operational monitoring and alerting.', 17, 'm_17_2', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A company is running multiple Amazon SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints. They need to ensure that their monthly AWS spend for these ML workloads does not exceed a predefined budget. The solution must proactively notify the finance and ML teams if the projected costs are trending towards exceeding the budget, to allow for timely intervention.', '["Set up an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm on the `EstimatedCharges` metric for the entire AWS account and send notifications via Amazon SNS.","Regularly review the AWS Cost Explorer dashboard manually at the end of each month to identify cost overruns.","Create an AWS Budget for the ML-related services, configured to send alerts via Amazon SNS when actual or forecasted costs exceed a threshold.","Implement a custom script that queries the AWS Billing API daily, calculates the spend, and emails the team if a threshold is met."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most appropriate and effective solution for proactive cost management. AWS Budgets allows you to set custom budgets that alert you when your costs or usage exceed (or are forecasted to exceed) your budgeted amount. It integrates directly with Amazon SNS for notifications, providing timely alerts to the relevant teams. Option A (incorrect) uses `EstimatedCharges` which is a good metric, but it applies to the entire account and may not be granular enough to specifically target ML workloads, making it harder to pinpoint the source of overspend. Option B (incorrect) is a reactive approach that only identifies overruns after the fact, which violates the ''proactively notify'' requirement. Option D (incorrect) involves custom development and maintenance for a script, which is less robust and more operationally intensive than using the managed AWS Budgets service.', 17, 'm_17_2', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A data science team frequently runs large-scale Amazon SageMaker training jobs for experimental models. These jobs are fault-tolerant and do not require immediate completion, but cost optimization is a critical factor. The team needs to train these models using the MOST cost-effective approach without compromising the ability to complete the training successfully.', '["Provision dedicated Amazon EC2 instances for training and manage the ML environment manually.","Use Amazon SageMaker On-Demand instances for all training jobs to ensure consistent performance.","Schedule training jobs to run during off-peak hours using Amazon SageMaker managed instances.","Configure Amazon SageMaker training jobs to use Spot Instances for significant cost savings."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most cost-effective approach for fault-tolerant Amazon SageMaker training jobs. SageMaker allows you to specify Spot Instances for training, which can provide significant cost savings (up to 90% compared to On-Demand instances) because they leverage unused EC2 capacity. Since the jobs are fault-tolerant and don''t require immediate completion, they can handle potential interruptions and resume, making Spot Instances ideal. Option A (incorrect) would significantly increase operational overhead for managing infrastructure and ML environments, and likely be less cost-effective than SageMaker''s managed service. Option B (incorrect) uses On-Demand instances, which are more expensive and do not meet the ''MOST cost-effective'' constraint for fault-tolerant workloads. Option C (incorrect) might offer some minor cost benefits if pricing varies by time, but it does not provide the substantial savings of Spot Instances and doesn''t inherently make the job more cost-effective in terms of instance pricing.', 17, 'm_17_2', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A company has deployed an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint that experiences variable traffic throughout the day. To ensure optimal performance and cost efficiency, the ML engineering team needs to monitor the endpoint''s resource utilization and automatically scale it up or down based on demand. Which Amazon Web Services (AWS) combination provides this capability with LEAST operational overhead?', '["Amazon SageMaker endpoint auto-scaling configured with Amazon CloudWatch alarms on metrics like `CPUUtilization` and `MemoryUtilization`.","A custom AWS Lambda function that periodically checks the endpoint''s `Invocations` metric and manually updates the endpoint configuration via the SageMaker API.","AWS Compute Optimizer recommendations applied manually to the SageMaker endpoint instances on a weekly basis.","Amazon CloudWatch Logs to capture endpoint access logs, then analyze them with Amazon Athena to identify peak usage patterns for manual scaling decisions."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most efficient solution with the least operational overhead. Amazon SageMaker supports auto-scaling for inference endpoints, which can be configured to automatically adjust the number of instances based on Amazon CloudWatch metrics such as `CPUUtilization`, `MemoryUtilization`, `InvocationsPerInstance`, or `ModelLatency`. This built-in feature handles scaling events automatically without manual intervention. Option B (incorrect) introduces custom development and maintenance overhead for the AWS Lambda function, which is more complex and less robust than SageMaker''s native auto-scaling. Option C (incorrect) provides recommendations but requires manual application and is not a real-time, automated scaling solution. Option D (incorrect) is a reactive, manual approach to identifying usage patterns and does not provide automated, real-time scaling, thus increasing operational overhead.', 17, 'm_17_2', 7, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_17_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A financial institution relies on a machine learning model deployed on an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint for real-time fraud detection. The data science team needs to proactively identify any significant shifts in the input data distribution that could lead to model degradation. They require an automated solution to detect data drift and trigger alerts.', '["Use AWS CloudTrail to log all SageMaker API calls and analyze these logs for unusual patterns that might indicate data drift.","Implement Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the SageMaker endpoint to monitor CPU utilization and network throughput, setting alarms if these infrastructure metrics deviate from baselines.","Develop a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically sample inference data from Amazon S3, run statistical tests for drift, and send emails via Amazon SNS if drift is detected.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor with a data quality monitoring schedule to analyze inference requests and detect drift, then integrate it with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for notifications."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most appropriate and automated solution for detecting data drift in Amazon SageMaker. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect data quality issues, including data drift, by comparing inference data against a baseline. It publishes metrics to Amazon CloudWatch, allowing CloudWatch Alarms to be configured for automated notifications when drift exceeds predefined thresholds. Option A (incorrect) is for auditing API calls, not for monitoring data distribution. Option B (incorrect) focuses on infrastructure metrics (CPU, throughput), which do not directly indicate data drift (Anti-pattern: ap-4.1-001). Option C (incorrect) would require significant custom development and maintenance, increasing operational overhead compared to the managed Model Monitor service.', 17, 'm_17_1', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A retail company has deployed a personalized product recommendation model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. To maintain fairness and model integrity, the data science team needs to continuously monitor the model for potential bias drift and unexpected shifts in feature attribution. They require an automated solution to track these ML-specific metrics.', '["Implement extensive AWS CloudTrail logging for all SageMaker inference requests, then manually review and analyze these detailed logs for complex patterns that might indicate subtle bias or feature attribution changes, which is a time-consuming process.","Periodically retrain the model on new data to inherently correct any bias or feature attribution shifts, without explicit monitoring.","Monitor the SageMaker endpoint''s latency and error rates using Amazon CloudWatch, as these metrics indirectly reflect model quality and bias.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to schedule bias drift and feature attribution drift monitoring jobs, which will analyze inference data and generate metrics in Amazon CloudWatch."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the dedicated and automated solution for monitoring bias drift and feature attribution drift. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor includes specific capabilities for detecting bias drift and feature attribution drift by analyzing inference data against a baseline. It publishes relevant metrics to Amazon CloudWatch, enabling automated tracking and alerting. Option A (incorrect) is for auditing API calls and would require complex, manual analysis to infer bias or feature attribution changes. Option B (incorrect) is a retraining strategy but does not provide continuous monitoring or alerts for drift detection. Option C (incorrect) monitors infrastructure-related operational metrics, not ML-specific metrics like bias or feature attribution drift (Anti-pattern: ap-4.1-001).', 17, 'm_17_1', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A financial institution uses an Amazon SageMaker model for fraud detection. The model''s accuracy is highly dependent on the consistency of incoming transaction data. The ML team needs to proactively detect any significant shifts in the distribution of the input features (data drift) in the production environment to prevent model performance degradation. The solution should provide automated alerts.', '["Implement a scheduled AWS Lambda function to sample incoming data, store it in Amazon S3, and run a custom statistical analysis script.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to analyze the data quality of incoming inference requests and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Manually review samples of production data daily and compare them against the training dataset using statistical tools.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms on infrastructure metrics like CPU utilization and network I/O for the SageMaker endpoint to detect anomalies."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate and efficient solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect data quality issues, including data drift, in production inference data. It can compare incoming data against a baseline and generate metrics that can trigger Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for automated alerts when significant shifts are detected. Option A (incorrect) requires custom development and maintenance for the AWS Lambda function and custom scripts, which increases operational overhead compared to a managed service like Model Monitor. Option C (incorrect) is a manual and reactive approach that is not scalable or proactive enough for critical fraud detection models. Option D (incorrect) focuses on infrastructure metrics, which is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.1-001]) for detecting model quality degradation due to data drift. An endpoint can be healthy infrastructure-wise while model accuracy degrades due to data drift.', 17, 'm_17_1', 10, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'An e-commerce company uses an Amazon SageMaker model to recommend products to customers in real-time. The model''s performance (e.g., accuracy, F1 score) is crucial for customer engagement. The ML team needs to continuously monitor the model''s quality in production and receive automated alerts if its performance degrades significantly. The solution should be integrated with existing AWS monitoring tools.', '["Set up an AWS Lambda function to periodically run batch inference on a test dataset and compare predictions against ground truth, then send emails.","Monitor Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the SageMaker endpoint''s CPU and memory utilization to infer model performance.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to evaluate model quality using ground truth data and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for alerts.","Implement AWS CloudTrail to log all SageMaker API calls and analyze these logs for any indications of model quality issues."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective solution for monitoring model quality. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can evaluate model quality by comparing inference predictions with ground truth labels collected from production. It generates model quality metrics that can be used to set up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms, providing automated alerts when performance degrades. Option A (incorrect) requires custom development and maintenance for the AWS Lambda function and custom logic, which is more complex than using SageMaker Model Monitor''s built-in capabilities. Option B (incorrect) focuses on infrastructure metrics, which is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.1-001]) because an endpoint can be healthy infrastructure-wise while model accuracy degrades. Option D (incorrect) is for auditing API calls and does not provide insights into model quality or performance degradation.', 17, 'm_17_1', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A media company uses an Amazon SageMaker model for content moderation. The model''s performance can degrade over time due to evolving internet slang and trends (concept drift). The ML team wants to implement a responsive retraining strategy that automatically triggers a new training job when significant model drift is detected, minimizing manual intervention and ensuring the model remains up-to-date.', '["Schedule a new Amazon SageMaker training job to run every month, regardless of model performance.","Manually review model performance metrics weekly and trigger retraining if a decline is observed.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch Alarms based on Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor''s drift detection metrics to trigger an AWS Lambda function, which then starts a new SageMaker training job.","Implement a system that retrains the model only when a user explicitly reports an incorrect moderation decision."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) provides the most automated and responsive retraining strategy. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can detect various types of drift (data, model quality, bias, feature attribution). By configuring Amazon CloudWatch Alarms on these drift metrics, an AWS Lambda function can be triggered to initiate a new SageMaker training job, effectively automating the retraining process based on actual drift detection. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.2-001]) because retraining on a fixed schedule may miss rapid drift or retrain unnecessarily, leading to suboptimal model performance or wasted resources. Option B (incorrect) is a manual and reactive approach that does not meet the requirement for automated and responsive retraining. Option D (incorrect) is highly reactive and relies on user feedback, which is too slow and inefficient for proactive model maintenance.', 17, 'm_17_1', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A healthcare provider uses an Amazon SageMaker model to predict patient risk factors. It is critical to ensure that the model does not exhibit unintended bias over time due to shifts in demographic data or feature distributions. The ML team needs a solution to continuously monitor for bias drift in the production environment and receive alerts if such drift is detected.', '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor''s bias drift detection capabilities, integrated with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Regularly collect production inference data and manually run Amazon SageMaker Clarify bias reports on a monthly basis.","Monitor the overall accuracy of the model using a separate validation dataset and assume bias if accuracy drops.","Implement custom scripts to calculate fairness metrics on the training data and compare them to the production data periodically."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most effective and operationally efficient solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor includes specific capabilities for detecting bias drift in production inference data. It can compare bias metrics against a baseline and trigger Amazon CloudWatch Alarms when significant shifts in bias are identified, providing automated and continuous monitoring. Option B (incorrect) involves manual effort and is not a continuous, automated monitoring solution, which could lead to delayed detection of bias drift. Option C (incorrect) is an indirect and unreliable method; a drop in accuracy does not necessarily indicate bias, and bias can exist even with high overall accuracy. Option D (incorrect) requires significant custom development and maintenance for scripts, which is more complex and less robust than using SageMaker Model Monitor''s built-in features.', 17, 'm_17_1', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A company uses an explainable AI model deployed on Amazon SageMaker to provide loan approval recommendations. Regulators require that the model''s decision-making process remains transparent and consistent. The ML team needs to detect if the relative importance of features (feature attribution) used by the model shifts significantly over time in production, as this could indicate a change in model behavior. The solution should provide automated alerts.', '["Implement a custom solution using AWS Lambda to periodically re-run SHAP or LIME explanations on production data and compare results.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect feature attribution drift and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Monitor the overall model accuracy and assume feature attribution drift if the accuracy drops below a certain threshold.","Regularly retrain the model on new data, assuming that retraining will inherently correct any feature attribution shifts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate and efficient solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor includes specific capabilities to detect feature attribution drift. It can compare the feature attributions of incoming inference requests against a baseline and generate metrics that can trigger Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for automated alerts when significant shifts are detected. This ensures continuous monitoring of model explainability. Option A (incorrect) requires significant custom development and maintenance for the AWS Lambda function and explanation libraries, increasing operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) is an indirect and unreliable method; a drop in accuracy does not directly indicate feature attribution drift, and feature attribution can shift without a noticeable immediate impact on overall accuracy. Option D (incorrect) is a reactive approach that does not provide proactive detection or alerts for feature attribution drift; retraining might address it, but without monitoring, the shift would go unnoticed until the next retraining cycle.', 17, 'm_17_1', 14, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_17_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A pharmaceutical company is training an Amazon SageMaker model using highly sensitive patient data. Due to strict regulatory compliance requirements, the training job must operate within a private network environment, without any direct access to the public internet, to prevent data exfiltration. All AWS service access must also be secured.', '["Configure the Amazon SageMaker training job to use a public subnet and rely on security groups to restrict inbound traffic.","Allow the Amazon SageMaker training job to access the public internet, but enable AWS CloudTrail to log all network activity.","Configure the Amazon SageMaker training job to run within a private Amazon VPC subnet with no public internet access, using VPC endpoints for AWS service connectivity.","Encrypt all training data in Amazon S3 with AWS KMS and assume this is sufficient to meet network isolation requirements."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the only solution that meets the strict compliance requirement for network isolation. Configuring the Amazon SageMaker training job to run within a private Amazon VPC subnet with no public internet access, and using VPC endpoints for AWS service connectivity (e.g., for Amazon S3, AWS KMS), ensures that all traffic remains within the private network, preventing data exfiltration and adhering to compliance. Option A (incorrect) uses a public subnet, which inherently allows public internet access, violating the ''no direct access to the public internet'' requirement. Option B (incorrect) is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.3-001]) as it explicitly allows direct internet access, which is unacceptable in regulated environments, even with logging. Option D (incorrect) addresses data at rest encryption but does not provide network isolation for the training job itself, which is a separate and critical security control.', 17, 'm_17_3', 15, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A data science team in a highly regulated industry is using Amazon SageMaker for model training and inference. All data used by SageMaker, including training datasets stored in Amazon S3, model artifacts, and inference data, must be encrypted at rest with customer-managed keys to meet compliance requirements. The solution should integrate seamlessly with SageMaker.', '["Manually encrypt all data files before uploading them to Amazon S3, then configure SageMaker to use these encrypted files.","Enable default Amazon S3 encryption with S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) for all buckets used by SageMaker.","Configure Amazon SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints to use an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) customer managed key (CMK) for encryption of data at rest.","Implement application-level encryption within the SageMaker training script to encrypt and decrypt data during processing."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective and integrated solution. Amazon SageMaker allows you to specify an AWS KMS customer managed key (CMK) for encrypting data at rest, including training data in Amazon S3, model artifacts, and inference data. This meets the requirement for customer-managed keys and integrates seamlessly with SageMaker''s managed services. Option A (incorrect) is a manual process that adds significant operational overhead and complexity, and it doesn''t cover encryption of model artifacts or inference data managed by SageMaker. Option B (incorrect) uses S3-managed keys (SSE-S3), which does not meet the requirement for *customer-managed* keys. Option D (incorrect) involves custom development, increases complexity within the ML code, and is generally less secure and harder to manage than leveraging AWS KMS for encryption at rest.', 17, 'm_17_3', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A machine learning engineering team needs to grant specific permissions to Amazon SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints to access necessary AWS resources, such as Amazon S3 buckets for data and model storage. The security team mandates that all permissions adhere strictly to the principle of least privilege. Which AWS service should be used to define and manage these permissions?', '["AWS IAM roles and policies attached to the SageMaker execution role.","Amazon VPC security groups configured to allow specific network traffic.","AWS CloudTrail for auditing all API calls made by SageMaker.","AWS Secrets Manager to store access keys for Amazon S3."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the appropriate service for defining and managing permissions based on the principle of least privilege. AWS IAM roles and policies allow you to specify exactly which AWS resources (e.g., specific Amazon S3 buckets, AWS KMS keys) a SageMaker training job or inference endpoint can access, and what actions it can perform. This ensures that SageMaker only has the necessary permissions. Option B (incorrect) manages network traffic rules, not permissions to access AWS resources. Option C (incorrect) is for auditing and logging API calls, not for granting or managing permissions. Option D (incorrect) is for securely storing credentials, but it does not define the permissions associated with those credentials or the SageMaker service itself.', 17, 'm_17_3', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'A data science team is developing an Amazon SageMaker training job that needs to access sensitive credentials for an external database. To comply with security best practices, these credentials must not be hardcoded in the training script or stored in plain text. The solution must provide a secure and managed way to retrieve these credentials during the training process.', '["Store the credentials directly in an Amazon S3 bucket and retrieve them from the training script.","Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the credentials and retrieve them programmatically within the SageMaker training job.","Embed the credentials as environment variables directly within the SageMaker training job definition.","Hardcode the credentials within the training script, relying on network isolation to protect them."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most secure and managed approach for handling sensitive credentials. AWS Secrets Manager allows you to securely store, manage, and retrieve database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle. SageMaker training jobs can be granted IAM permissions to retrieve these secrets programmatically, ensuring credentials are not hardcoded or exposed in plain text. Option A (incorrect) is insecure as Amazon S3 is not designed for secure credential storage and retrieval without additional layers of security. Option C (incorrect) is insecure because environment variables can be easily inspected and are not suitable for sensitive credentials. Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern and a severe security vulnerability, as hardcoding credentials makes them difficult to rotate and highly susceptible to compromise.', 17, 'm_17_3', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_14_all_1774923300597', 'An organization has deployed an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint that processes sensitive customer data. To meet stringent security and compliance requirements, the endpoint must only be accessible from within the organization''s private network, and all outbound traffic from the endpoint must also be restricted to specific internal resources. The solution needs to ensure network isolation.', '["Configure the Amazon SageMaker endpoint to use a public IP address and rely on security groups to filter inbound traffic.","Encrypt all data transmitted to and from the SageMaker endpoint using TLS, assuming this provides sufficient network isolation.","Deploy the Amazon SageMaker inference endpoint within a private Amazon VPC subnet, configuring security groups and network ACLs for traffic control.","Use AWS CloudTrail to monitor all network connections to the SageMaker endpoint and alert on unauthorized access attempts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective solution for achieving network isolation for an Amazon SageMaker inference endpoint. Deploying the endpoint within a private Amazon VPC subnet ensures that it is not accessible from the public internet. Security groups and network ACLs within the VPC can then be used to precisely control inbound and outbound traffic, restricting access to only authorized internal resources. Option A (incorrect) uses a public IP address, which exposes the endpoint to the public internet, violating the requirement for private network access. Option B (incorrect) addresses encryption in transit but does not provide network isolation; TLS encrypts data but doesn''t prevent unauthorized network access. Option D (incorrect) is for auditing and logging, which is a reactive measure and does not prevent unauthorized network access or enforce network isolation.', 17, 'm_17_3', 19, NULL, NULL);

